I split the drifted price rows into live and dormant, and corrected the three live OpenAI rows. Commit 9c8145d7 is "Part of #1012" and the breakdown is posted on #1012. Build, test, clippy and fmt all pass.

Measurement. Today's sweep started at drifted 70, not the 73 from the 2026-10-20 release gate; I didn't trace that difference.
• Before: compared 114, matched 31, drifted 70, cache_read_only 13, unpriced 60 of 174
• After: compared 114, matched 35, drifted 66, cache_read_only 13, unpriced 60 of 174

Split. A row counts as live if a user can pick it from the known-model list or gets it as a provider default. 10 of the 70 drifted rows are live: google 4, openai 3, mistral 2, deepseek 1. xai and zai have none. The other 60 are only reached through loose name-matching rules in the price table (TTS, image, preview and dated variants).

Fix. OpenAI had the most live rows that yoyo prices itself, so I set gpt-5, gpt-5-mini and gpt-5.5 to models.dev's prices, including the cached-input rate. Before this, cached tokens were billed as free: yoagent takes cached tokens out of the input count, and yoyo's cache rate for these models was 0.0. These were value changes only, and cost.rs stays at 3613 lines.
• Why drift fell by 4, not 3: gpt-5.1 goes through the same price rule as gpt-5, and models.dev gives it exactly gpt-5's price. I compared the drifted lists before and after: those 4 rows left, and no row newly drifted.
• New test: it checks all three prices through the same lookup /cost uses, and checks that the ids are still pickable. I put the old gpt-5 price back temporarily and the test failed naming gpt-5; restoring the fix made it pass again.
• One file outside the plan: src/openai_preset_tests.rs had a test pinning gpt-5's old price. I updated that number; what it checks is unchanged.

No upstream issue filed. The two live Google rows priced by yoagent (gemini-3.8-flash, gemini-4-argon) aren't a yoagent bug. Its price file uses Google's regular price on purpose, as an upper bound, and its own note says so. models.dev lists the introductory prices, which end on 2026-11-30 (gemini-4-argon) and 2026-12-31 (gemini-3.8-flash).

What's next, as posted on #1012:
• Google: the two gemini-2.5-flash rows need a separate price rule for flash-lite, about 3 more lines in cost.rs, which is already 8 lines over its 3605 cap.
• Mistral: mistral-large-latest is a simple value change. mistral-small-latest needs its own price rule, because changing the shared one would break mistral-small-2506, which currently matches.
• Deepseek: deepseek-v4-pro is still on the recorded list of known disagreements, because changing its price moves the default for every DeepSeek user.
